WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:03.960
A lack of sleep is dangerous.

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:10.160
We’re often told not to drive whilst tired
and falling asleep at the wheel can be fatal.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:15.710
During World War II many sleep-deprived pilots
fell asleep mid-flight and crashed their plane

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:18.310
on the way home from the war zone.

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:24.820
In fact, you may be horrified to hear that
in a study, 56% of consumer aircraft pilots

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:29.700
admitted to falling asleep in the cockpit
whilst the plane was in autopilot.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:35.930
It’s fair to say that our body needs sleep
but how long could you theoretically stay

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:37.320
awake for?

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.180
Could a person die if they were forced to
stay awake for a long time?

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:45.750
And what happens to your mind and your body
when you miss out on sleep?

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:51.760
Let’s find out.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.510
If you live to 78 you will have spent around
25 years asleep.

00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:58.989
But what if you can’t sleep?

00:00:58.989 --> 00:01:01.680
Well It’s not as simple as that.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.220
Eventually your brain will make you sleep.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:08.870
After only a couple of days the urge to sleep
will become greater than the urge to eat.

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:13.700
At this point you might experience a phenomenon
known as microsleep.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:20.080
This is when your brain forces you to sleep
for a short time without you being aware.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:23.240
Microsleep is a common symptom amongst insomnia
sufferers.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:26.860
Your brain suddenly shuts down, but your eyes
remain open.

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:30.979
It can last for a few seconds or a few minutes.

00:01:30.979 --> 00:01:36.840
During microsleep the person’s eyes usually
remain wide open but they will have a blank,

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.119
distant stare.

00:01:38.119 --> 00:01:43.150
They will go into a brief, zombie-like state
and won’t respond to any outside information.

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:47.530
You’ve probably experienced microsleep,
if you’ve ever drove for a long period of

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:51.180
time on the motorway or highway, on very little
sleep.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:55.360
It’s that feeling when you blank out for
a couple of seconds and it feels like you

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:00.280
lost a brief period of time but you don’t
remember actually falling asleep.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:05.510
As you can probably imagine, microsleep is
the cause of many accidents.

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:08.899
Microsleep is your brain’s way of going
“Okay dude I know you’re trying to stay

00:02:08.899 --> 00:02:14.219
awake at the moment, but I’m really tired,
you carry on as you are, I’m just gonna

00:02:14.219 --> 00:02:16.299
catch a few z’s for a minute.

00:02:16.299 --> 00:02:17.299
Okay?”

00:02:17.299 --> 00:02:20.859
But when it comes to wanting to sleep, some
people don’t have a choice.

00:02:20.859 --> 00:02:27.790
A rare genetic disease called Fatal Familial
Insomnia or FFI, prevents people falling asleep

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:29.269
at all.

00:02:29.269 --> 00:02:32.489
It affects around just forty families worldwide.

00:02:32.489 --> 00:02:38.629
The disease destroys the area of the brain
that controls when we sleep, the thalamus.

00:02:38.629 --> 00:02:43.650
Sufferers of FFI will experience progressively
worse insomnia, after a while they start to

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:49.680
hallucinate and eventually they will because
extremely confused, similar to dementia patients,

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:54.219
and then, they will eventually die.

00:02:54.219 --> 00:02:58.930
The average lifespan for someone with this
tragic and scary disease is just eighteen

00:02:58.930 --> 00:02:59.930
months.

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:04.779
But even for the regular person a lack of
sleep can have severe effects on the body

00:03:04.779 --> 00:03:07.199
and can even be fatal.

00:03:07.199 --> 00:03:10.980
After missing just one night of sleep the
first thing you will notice is a decrease

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:12.799
in cognitive function.

00:03:12.799 --> 00:03:18.010
First and foremost, a lack of sleep impairs
attention, concentration, reasoning, problem

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:21.120
solving and your ability to learn.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:26.389
But perhaps what’s even scarier is that
by missing out on sleep you can wipe whole

00:03:26.389 --> 00:03:28.269
days from your memory.

00:03:28.269 --> 00:03:33.530
It’s believed that during sleep our brain
consolidates, organises and permanently stores

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:36.699
that day’s memories and what we learnt.

00:03:36.699 --> 00:03:40.900
If you don’t get enough sleep, instead of
storing those memories forever, your brain

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:43.249
will simply forget them altogether.

00:03:43.249 --> 00:03:46.459
But that’s only scratching the surface.

00:03:46.459 --> 00:03:50.959
Continual sleep loss over a period of several
days or more can have substantially worse

00:03:50.959 --> 00:03:53.029
effects on the body.

00:03:53.029 --> 00:03:58.489
Chronic sleep loss or insomnia has been linked
to a significant increase in heart disease,

00:03:58.489 --> 00:04:02.389
strokes, diabetes, obesity and depression.

00:04:02.389 --> 00:04:08.449
In fact, a study showed that over 90% of people
with insomnia also suffer from another serious

00:04:08.449 --> 00:04:09.449
health condition.

00:04:09.449 --> 00:04:12.359
Oh and sleep also keeps you looking young.

00:04:12.359 --> 00:04:17.079
It’s during sleep that the growth hormone
is released that repairs tissue, keeping those

00:04:17.079 --> 00:04:18.600
wrinkles at bay.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:23.050
When you don’t get much sleep, specifically
slow-wave sleep, your body doesn’t get an

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:28.250
opportunity to repair itself, so you age faster.

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:33.700
Not only that but when you miss out on sleep
your body releases the stress hormone cortisol

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:38.700
which actually breaks down skin collagen,
the protein that keeps your skin smooth, bouncy

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:41.150
and looking mighty fine.

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:46.820
Sleep loss, even a few missed hours per night,
causes droopy, yellowish skin, puffy eyes,

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:49.670
fine lines and dark circles.

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:53.700
So now you know why you really, really shouldn’t
miss out on sleep.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:58.940
But how long could you, theoretically stay
awake?

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:03.680
If you were forced to stay awake by an evil
scientist who strapped you to a chair, Clockwork

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:08.970
Orange-style, and gave you a quick electric
shock every time you started to drift, how

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:14.010
long would it take before your body gave up,
and what would be the consequence?

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:24.140
The answer to the question how long can humans
stay awake for is 264 hours, or about 11 days.

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:30.680
In 1947 a 16-year-old high school student
in San Diego, California named Randy Gardner

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:37.090
set the world record for the longest scientifically
documented case of someone going without sleep,

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:39.020
without using stimulants.

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:43.460
Gardner stayed awake for 264 hours, or 11
days.

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:48.830
Before Gardner’s attempt there were many
attempt to stay awake for a prolonged period

00:05:48.830 --> 00:05:51.890
and since then there have been many more.

00:05:51.890 --> 00:05:57.300
The current record holder is Tony Wright,
a man from the south of England who broke

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:04.270
Garner’s record by just two hours, staying
awake for 266 hours.

00:06:04.270 --> 00:06:06.250
Noticing the trend here?

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:11.260
Almost everyone who’s attempted to break
the record hits a road block at around 11

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:12.260
days.

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:14.110
That seems to be the bodies limit.

00:06:14.110 --> 00:06:18.360
In fact, many researchers have attempted to
stay awake over the years and the average

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:21.970
period they reach is eight to ten days.

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:26.690
After which the urge to sleep becomes far
too overwhelming, to continue fighting against.

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:31.080
There haven’t been any recorded cases of
someone staying awake for longer than eleven

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:32.470
consecutive days.

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:36.440
But if they did, could it result in death?

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:40.830
In the sleep laboratory at the University
of Chicago an experiment was conducted on

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:44.750
rats to find out if sleep deprivation can
kill.

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:49.150
Rats were put on a rotating disc which was
suspended over a pool of water.

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:53.360
Sensors were attached to the rats to record
their brain waves and a computer program was

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:59.180
setup to analyse those brain waves and detect
when the rats were about to fall asleep.

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:04.860
At which point the disc would spin faster,
meaning if the rats fell asleep and didn’t

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:09.740
steady themselves on the rapidly spinning
disc, they would inevitably fall into the

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:11.240
water below.

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:16.090
This harsh treatment prevented the rats from
sleeping at all, for risk of falling into

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:17.090
the water.

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:22.590
I know, us humans do some rather horrendous
things in the pursuit of knowledge.

00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:26.250
So was this horrific experiment worth anything?

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:27.720
What did we learn?

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:33.360
Well, all the rats that underwent the experiment
eventually died from a lack of sleep.

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:37.340
The average lifespan of the rats was 11-32
days.

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:42.530
The scientists conducting the experiment believed
the cause of death to be whole body hypermetabolism,

00:07:42.530 --> 00:07:48.420
a condition which causes extreme weight loss,
and if not remedied, death.

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:52.340
The hypermetabolism was most likely brought
on by the lack of sleep.

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:56.940
It’s highly likely that if a human were
to stay awake for much longer than eleven

00:07:56.940 --> 00:08:03.260
days then hypermetabolism would kick in and
cause one’s eventual demise.

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:07.360
Or some other equally extreme condition brought
on by sleeplessness.

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:12.150
Although it’s believed to have happened
at some point in history, after all, sleep-deprivation

00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:14.250
has been used as a method of torture.

00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:20.570
There has never been a case well documented
enough, to study the effects of forcing someone

00:08:20.570 --> 00:08:26.050
to stay awake and so, scientists aren’t
exactly sure what would happen.

00:08:26.050 --> 00:08:31.060
But, just like the rats, the general consensus
is that if one were to stay awake for much

00:08:31.060 --> 00:08:36.099
longer than eleven consecutive days, they
would most certainly die.

00:08:36.099 --> 00:08:40.960
The cause of death wouldn’t be sleeplessness,
there’s no such condition, it would be some

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:44.229
horrific side-effect brought on from not sleeping.

00:08:44.229 --> 00:08:48.570
For example, hypermetabolism, as was the case
with the rats.

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:54.601
Although, the body’s urge to sleep is so
strong that this almost never happens, so

00:08:54.601 --> 00:08:59.569
there is very little evidence to be able to
know for certain what exactly would be the

00:08:59.569 --> 00:09:03.240
cause of death, at least in the case of humans.

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:08.620
But, just to be safe, try not to miss out
on your precious beauty sleep, because if

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:37.160
you do it enough, it very well, could, kill
you.

